{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/motherhood",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "motherhood"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "A socially and culturally constructed status, identity, and set of practices related to giving birth, raising and caring for a child, as well as to assigning women responsibility for the child’s well-being."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "It is not merely a consequence of biological childbirth, but a relationship and social role shaped by gender norms, ideals of the “good mother,” care arrangements, family relations, public policies, and access to resources and support. Motherhood includes both women’s experience and self-identification as mothers, as well as care practices and social expectations concerning who should provide care and how. It is also linked to reproductive and family rights, as cultural and institutional norms may limit women’s recognition as potential or competent mothers. Operationally, motherhood can be analysed through pathways to motherhood, reproductive aspirations and decisions, the organisation of everyday care, maternal identity and meanings, social expectations, access to support and services, and the ability to exercise parental rights. This approach is consistent with Article 23 of the CRPD and General Comment No. 3, which stress the equal right of persons with disabilities to family life and parenthood and the duty to provide appropriate support."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "11.5"
  ],
  "related": [
    {
      "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility",
